      },
      {
        "name": "README.md",
        "type": "file",
        "content": "# 🛠️ C Systems Programming & Memory Mastery Track\n\nWelcome to the **C Systems Programming & Memory Mastery Track** materialized by **Trak**.\n\nThis workspace is an in-depth curriculum covering low-level memory allocation (`malloc`/`free`), pointer arithmetic, data structure alignment, POSIX system calls (`fork`, `exec`), `pthreads`, and defensive systems engineering.\n\n## 📚 Syllabus & Module Map\n\n| Module | Title | Key Topics & Internals |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `00-setup-and-toolchain` | Toolchain & Compilation Pipeline | GCC/Clang, Makefiles, Preprocessor (`-E`), Assembler (`-S`), Linker (`-o`) |\n| `01-basics-types-and-memory` | Exact-Width Types & Endianness | `<stdint.h>`, `size_t`, `ptrdiff_t`, Big-Endian vs Little-Endian bytes |\n| `02-pointers-and-address-arithmetic` | Pointers & Memory Addresses | Dereference (`*`), Address-of (`&`), pointer scaling arithmetic, `void*` |\n| `03-memory-allocation-malloc-free` | Dynamic Heap Management | `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `free`, fragmentation, use-after-free |\n| `04-arrays-and-pointer-decay` | Array Decay & Multi-dim Pointers | Array to pointer decay, `char* argv[]`, pointer vs array index arithmetic |\n| `05-strings-and-buffer-safety` | Strings & Buffer Safety | Null terminator (`\\0`), `snprintf`, safe string bounds, `strlen` vs `sizeof` |\n| `06-structs-unions-and-alignment` | Struct Packing & Memory Alignment | Padding bytes, `__attribute__((packed))`, unions for type punning, bitfields |\n| `07-bitwise-operations-and-masks` | Bitwise Manipulation & Flags | Bitwise operators (`&`, `|`, `^`, `~`, `<<`, `>>`), mask flags, bit clearing |\n| `08-functions-and-function-pointers` | Function Pointers & Callbacks | Callback dispatch architectures, jump tables, event handlers |\n| `09-preprocessor-and-macros` | Preprocessor Metaprogramming | `#define`, stringification (`#`), token pasting (`##`), header guards |\n| `10-file-io-and-streams` | Standard File Streams | `fopen`, `fread`, `fwrite`, `fclose`, binary vs text mode, buffering |\n| `11-posix-system-calls-and-file-descriptors` | POSIX Low-Level I/O | `open`, `read`, `write`, `close`, file descriptors (0, 1, 2), `lseek` |\n| `12-processes-and-signals` | Processes & Signal Handlers | `fork()`, `execve()`, `waitpid()`, zombie processes, `sigaction` |\n| `13-posix-threads-pthreads` | Multithreading with pthreads | `pthread_create`, `pthread_join`, `pthread_mutex_t`, race conditions |\n| `14-dynamic-data-structures` | Dynamic Data Structures | Linked lists, growable dynamic vectors, hash tables in pure C |\n| `15-shared-libraries-and-linking` | Dynamic Linking & dlsym | Static (`.a`) vs Shared (`.so`), `dlopen`, `dlsym`, symbol resolution |\n| `16-debugging-with-gdb-and-valgrind` | Debugging & Memory Profiling | GDB stack backtraces, Valgrind Memcheck for invalid reads/writes |\n| `17-safe-c-best-practices` | Defensive Systems Engineering | Integer overflow prevention, `goto cleanup` idiom, null validation |\n| `18-interview-questions-and-drills` | Interview Mastery Drills | Top 10 C gotchas (Dangling pointers, sequence points, alignment faults) |\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ How to Practice\nEach module contains:\n- `README.md` — Deep theoretical explanation and architecture notes.\n- `example.c` — Fully runnable concept demonstration.\n- `exercise/starter.c` — Hands-on problem with `TODO` markers.\n- `exercise/solution.c` — Complete reference solution.\n"
      },
      {
        "name": "00-setup-and-toolchain",
        "type": "directory",
        "children": [
          {
            "name": "README.md",
            "type": "file",
            "content": "# 00 — C Compilation Pipeline & Toolchain\n\n## 🎯 The 4 Compilation Stages\n1. **Preprocessing (`cpp`)**: Expands `#include`, `#define`, strips comments (`gcc -E`).\n2. **Compilation (`cc1`)**: Translates C to assembly code (`gcc -S`).\n3. **Assembly (`as`)**: Translates assembly to machine object code (`gcc -c`).\n4. **Linking (`ld`)**: Combines object files and libraries into executable binary (`gcc -o`).\n"
          },
          {
            "name": "example.c",
            "type": "file",
            "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"=== 00: C Compilation Pipeline Verified ===\\n\");\n    #ifdef __GNUC__\n    printf(\"Compiler: GCC %d.%d.%d\\n\", __GNUC__, __GNUC_MINOR__, __GNUC_PATCHLEVEL__);\n    #endif\n    return 0;\n}\n"
          },
          {
            "name": "exercise",
            "type": "directory",
            "children": [
              {
                "name": "starter.c",
                "type": "file",
                "content": "// TODO: Write a program that prints the sizeof all fundamental C types in bytes.\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    // FILL HERE\n    return 0;\n}\n"
              },
              {
                "name": "solution.c",
