        "name": "05-strings-and-buffer-safety",
        "type": "directory",
        "children": [
          {
            "name": "README.md",
            "type": "file",
            "content": "# 05 — Strings & Buffer Overflow Safety\n\n## 🎯 Null-Terminated Strings\nC strings are character arrays ending in `\\0`. Never use unsafe functions like `strcpy` or `gets`; always use `snprintf`.\n"
          },
          {
            "name": "example.c",
            "type": "file",
            "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char buffer[32];\n    snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"Track: %s | Version: %d.%d\", \"C23\", 1, 2);\n    printf(\"Safe formatted string: %s\\n\", buffer);\n    return 0;\n}\n"
          },
          {
            "name": "exercise",
            "type": "directory",
            "children": [
              {
                "name": "starter.c",
                "type": "file",
                "content": "// TODO: Implement a safe string concatenation function.\n"
              },
              {
                "name": "solution.c",
                "type": "file",
                "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char dest[64] = \"Hello, \";\n    strncat(dest, \"World from C Systems!\", sizeof(dest) - strlen(dest) - 1);\n    printf(\"Result: %s\\n\", dest);\n    return 0;\n}\n"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
